libairspyhf: wait for cancelled transfers, and retune between sample blocks

A stop cancelled the transfers, polled for events at most once per transfer
with a 50 us timeout, joined the transfer and consumer threads, and polled
once more with a zero timeout. libusb calls a cancelled transfer back later
with LIBUSB_TRANSFER_CANCELLED and owns it and its buffer until then, so
transfers could still be in flight when airspyhf_stop() returned.
airspyhf_close() then freed them, and a restart (airspyhf_start() after a
stop, as a sample-rate change does) resubmitted them: freeing a transfer
libusb still owns is undefined behaviour, and libusb refuses to submit one
as busy. A failed submission at start left the transfers already submitted
the same way, and left the device marked as streaming with no threads, so
the next start failed too.

The device already counted its live transfers, but on plain fields: the
count was read on the caller's thread and changed by callbacks on whichever
thread handles events, and a callback dropped it before resubmitting, so it
could touch zero while a transfer was on its way back to libusb. The count
is now atomic. A start reserves the whole batch before the first
submission, and a failed submission gives back its own slot and those of
the transfers never submitted. The callback keeps the slot when it
resubmits; any other outcome (stopping, a failed status or resubmission)
gives it back as its last touch of the device. A start that fails after
submitting some of the batch (a refused submission, or a thread that cannot
start) takes them back before it returns, as a stop would, so it leaves the
device idle and a start can follow at once. The stop's bounded polls are
replaced by a wait: cancel the transfers and handle events until the count
is zero, with no timeout, since libusb guarantees each callback. Each pass
cancels again, in case a callback on another thread resubmitted before it
saw the stop; a poll that keeps failing backs off 1 ms instead of spinning.

A stream that ended by itself (a failed transfer or event poll, or the
sample callback asking to stop) left its threads unjoined and its other
transfers in flight. airspyhf_start() now reaps it first, as a stop would,
before it resets any state or resubmits anything.

Retuning while streaming raced the consumer thread. airspyhf_set_freq() and
the other setters (calibration, sample rate, the IQ correction point and
balancer settings, the DSP switch) write the fine-tuning shift, sample rate,
filter gain and DSP flags, and reset or reconfigure the IQ balancer, while the
consumer reads those fields and runs the balancer on every block, all on
plain fields. A new mutex, held by the consumer while it converts a block and
by each setter while it changes what the conversion reads, puts every change
between two blocks; airspyhf_set_freq_double() hands the balancer reset and
the new shift over together. The setters take it only around those writes,
never across a USB request, so a slow control transfer never holds up the
samples, and the consumer pays one uncontended lock per block.

streaming and stop_requested become atomic too. The caller's stop, the
transfer thread, the consumer thread and the callbacks all read and write
them, which was a data race on plain volatile fields; ThreadSanitizer
reports it as soon as a test drives the library's own threads.

Every access to the count and the two flags goes through a small shim:
C11 <stdatomic.h>, or the Interlocked functions on MSVC, whose C compiler
has no <stdatomic.h> without /experimental:c11atomics. dsd-neo applies this
patch to the libairspyhf it builds for its Windows release. airspyhf.c has
CRLF line endings, which the patch keeps.

Generated against airspyhf 24fe8ffcb00b14f827268bbad89ae1392de055e5.

